===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=vehicle|:inh|enm:cart<ety:bor<non:kartr>:der<ang:cræt>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|cart}}, {{m|enm|kart}}, from {{der|en|non|kartr|t=wagon; cart}},<ref>[http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cart Etymology] in Merriam-Webster's dictionay</ref> merged with native {{cog|ang|cræt|t=a chariot; cart}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*krattaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*krattijô}}, {{m|gem-pro|*kradō}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gret-|t=tracery; wattle; cradle; cage; basket}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*ger-|t=to turn, wind}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fy|kret|t=wheelbarrow for hauling dung}}, {{cog|nl|krat}}, {{m|nl|kret|t=crate; wheelbarrow for hauling dung}}, {{cog|de|Krätze|t=basket; pannier}}. Wider cognates include {{cog|sa|ग्रन्थ|t=a binding}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A small, [[open]], [[wheel]]ed vehicle, drawn or pushed by a person or animal, often with two wheels on one axle, more often used for [[transport]]ing goods than passengers.
#: {{syn|en|kart}}
#: {{cot|en|wagon|q=usually with four wheels on two axles}}
#: {{ux|en|The grocer delivered his goods by '''cart'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=We made an odd party before the arrival of the Ten, particularly when the Celebrity dropped in for lunch or dinner. He could not be induced to remain permanently at Mohair because Miss Trevor was at Asquith, but he appropriated a Hempstead '''cart''' from the Mohair stables and made the trip sometimes twice in a day.}}
# A small [[motor]] [[vehicle]] resembling a car; a [[go-cart]].
# {{lb|en|Internet}} A [[shopping cart]].
